---
import DataPlate from "../components/DataPlate.astro";
import RuleDatum from "../components/RuleDatum.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  BUILD_SHA,
  CONTENT_VERSION,
  COPYRIGHT_YEAR,
} from "../lib/site-identity";

const description =
  "Engineering case study for IS0 Null: a static Astro photography archive designed around zero client JavaScript, build-time image delivery, metadata privacy, and explicit verification.";
---

<Base
  title="Engineering case study — IS0 Null"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/case-study/"
  currentPath="/case-study/"
  lang="en"
  buildSha={BUILD_SHA}
  contentVersion={CONTENT_VERSION}
  copyrightYear={COPYRIGHT_YEAR}
>
  <article class="case-study container-prose">
    <header class="case-study__header">
      <p class="case-study__overline">IS0 Null / ENGINEERING</p>
      <h1>Engineering a photography archive with no client runtime</h1>

      <p class="case-study__lede">
        IS0 Null is a static photography portfolio and limited-edition print
        storefront. The system treats image delivery, metadata privacy,
        accessible viewing, and commercial correctness as one engineering
        problem rather than as independent frontend features.
      </p>

      <DataPlate class="case-study__facts">
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Design and engineering, Ross Todd</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Stack</dt>
            <dd>Astro 7, TypeScript, Sharp, native HTML and CSS</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Rendering</dt>
            <dd>Static HTML, eight generated routes</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Client runtime</dt>
            <dd>Zero JavaScript assets in the verified build</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Commerce</dt>
            <dd>Test-mode demonstration; production sales disabled</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </DataPlate>
    </header>

    <RuleDatum as="div">
      <div class="case-study__sections">
        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="premise-heading">
          <h2 id="premise-heading">The premise</h2>

          <p>
            A photography archive has little client state. Its content is fixed
            at publication, its primary interactions are viewing a larger image
            and moving to the next one, and the largest transfer is always the
            photography itself. A framework runtime would therefore compete with
            the content it exists to present.
          </p>

          <p>
            The resulting constraint is exact: gallery routes ship no client
            JavaScript. The target is not a small bundle or a viewer that
            hydrates on demand. The build must emit no JavaScript asset at all.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          class="case-study__section"
          aria-labelledby="implemented-heading"
        >
          <h2 id="implemented-heading">The implemented slice</h2>

          <p>
            The current repository statically compiles the homepage and seven
            destination routes. Astro has no UI-framework integrations, fonts
            are self-hosted, and the homepage hero passes explicit intrinsic
            dimensions and responsive source generation through Astro’s Sharp
            image service.
          </p>

          <p>
            Build identity is deterministic rather than time-derived. The full
            source revision and content version are emitted as document
            metadata, while the footer exposes an abbreviated revision for a
            human-readable release reference. The stacked IS0 Null wordmark uses
            one semantic component for compact and display contexts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          class="case-study__section"
          aria-labelledby="architecture-heading"
        >
          <h2 id="architecture-heading">Architecture</h2>

          <p>
            Astro is used for static route generation, content validation, and
            build-time image processing—not for hydration. The integrations
            array remains empty, which removes the mechanism that would make a
            framework island possible. Every production route becomes an HTML
            file served by a CDN-backed host.
          </p>

          <p>
            This model keeps runtime secrets, databases, sessions, and
            migrations outside the public site. Rollback is a previous immutable
            build. The remaining operational surface is cache behavior, security
            headers, and the integrity of generated artifacts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          class="case-study__section"
          aria-labelledby="interaction-heading"
        >
          <h2 id="interaction-heading">The interaction trade-off</h2>

          <p>
            The planned gallery viewer uses the native Popover API. Each
            thumbnail targets a build-rendered popover, so the browser supplies
            opening, Escape handling, light dismiss, and top-layer placement
            without event listeners. Previous and next controls remain ordinary
            buttons.
          </p>

          <p>
            That decision has a real accessibility cost. A popover is not a
            modal dialog: it does not make the document inert or trap focus.
            Keyboard navigation remains operable but not arrow-key fast. If
            manual testing shows that mitigation is insufficient, the fallback
            is a plain link to a larger derivative—not a concealed hydration
            boundary. The constraint remains credible only when its cost is
            stated before release.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="model-heading">
          <h2 id="model-heading">Content that fails loudly</h2>

          <p>
            The planned content schema makes invalid publishing states
            unrepresentable. Alt text must contain non-whitespace text. EXIF
            values remain measured data—an aperture is stored as a number, not
            as a preformatted string. Formatting belongs to the presentation
            layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Print availability is represented by the presence of a complete
            offer object rather than by a boolean beside optional price, SKU,
            dimensions, and edition fields. A print route can only exist when
            the data required to sell the print exists with it. Cross-entry
            checks then enforce SKU uniqueness and original-object resolution.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="assets-heading">
          <h2 id="assets-heading">Protection without theatre</h2>

          <p>
            The valuable files never belong in the public build. Originals
            remain in private object storage; the site receives viewing
            derivatives with a bounded long edge. The metadata pipeline is
            designed to remove GPS and other unnecessary source data while
            re-embedding creator and copyright attribution that survives a
            downloaded derivative.
          </p>

          <p>
            Right-click blocking, drag suppression, overlay divs, and
            developer-tool detection are intentionally absent. They are bypassed
            through the network panel, break expected browser behavior, and
            still cannot stop a screenshot. Protection starts by declining to
            deliver the original.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="commerce-heading">
          <h2 id="commerce-heading">Commerce behind a narrow boundary</h2>

          <p>
            The first commerce design uses provider-hosted checkout links so
            card data never reaches the static site. The provider name is
            isolated behind one URL resolver, keeping product templates
            independent of Stripe and preserving a migration path to
            server-created sessions when inventory requirements justify a
            runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            Commerce remains test-mode only. Production activation requires
            complete trader details, shipping and tax facts, a legally reviewed
            withdrawal notice, and the electronic withdrawal function now
            required for online distance contracts in Germany. The technical
            demo is not represented as a completed sale.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section
          class="case-study__section"
          aria-labelledby="verification-heading"
        >
          <h2 id="verification-heading">Verification is the deliverable</h2>

          <p>
            Performance, privacy, accessibility, and asset-protection claims are
            divided into three states: enforced automatically, measured and
            recorded, or explicitly unverified. The current output guard walks
            every generated route, rejects script elements, hydration
            directives, Astro islands, inline handlers, and JavaScript assets,
            and verifies the route inventory and homepage metadata.
          </p>

          <p>
            The branding contract receives an independent source and asset
            check. It verifies the governed component, exact equal-width line
            geometry, self-hosted font weights, SVG outline exports, PNG
            dimensions, checksums, and production-output parity. Field
            performance values remain absent until measurements exist.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="status-heading">
          <h2 id="status-heading">Current status</h2>

          <p>
            The homepage, shared layout, navigation, footer, wordmark system,
            build identity, and legal/editorial shells are implemented. The
            collection- backed gallery templates, declarative viewer, EXIF
            extraction and re-embedding pipeline, metadata integrity scan, print
            routes, production checkout, and field performance record remain
            implementation work.
          </p>

          <p>
            This boundary is deliberate. A portfolio case study loses
            credibility when it presents target architecture as delivered
            behavior. The page is updated when a repository check or recorded
            measurement can support the next claim.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="author-heading">
          <h2 id="author-heading">Author</h2>

          <p>
            Ross Todd is a senior software engineer based in Munich. IS0 Null is
            a sole-authored portfolio project spanning product definition,
            visual design, content modelling, frontend architecture, build
            tooling, accessibility, security, and release verification.
          </p>

          <p><a href="https://github.com/rsstdd">GitHub profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="case-study__section" aria-labelledby="revision-heading">
          <h2 id="revision-heading">Revision</h2>
          <p><time datetime="2026-08-03">3 August 2026</time></p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </RuleDatum>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study {
    padding-block: var(--space-16) 0;
  }

  .case-study__header {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-16);
  }

  .case-study__overline {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-data);
    font-feature-settings: "zero";
    font-size: var(--text-overline);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-overline);
    line-height: var(--leading-overline);
  }

  .case-study h1,
  .case-study h2 {
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .case-study h1 {
    max-inline-size: 18ch;
    font-size: var(--text-display);
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
  }

  .case-study h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    line-height: var(--leading-h2);
  }

  .case-study__lede {
    max-inline-size: 60ch;
    font-size: var(--text-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-body-lg);
  }

  .case-study__facts {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
  }

  .case-study__facts dl,
  .case-study__facts div {
    display: grid;
  }

  .case-study__facts dl {
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .case-study__facts div {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(7rem, auto) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-study__facts dt {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .case-study__facts dd {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .case-study__sections,
  .case-study__section {
    display: grid;
  }

  .case-study__sections {
    gap: var(--space-10);
  }

  .case-study__section {
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-study__section + .case-study__section {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-10);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border-c);
  }

  .case-study__section p {
    max-inline-size: 65ch;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .case-study__facts div {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
